<Project>

  <!--
    Central Package Management.

    Every package version for the whole solution lives here. A csproj declares
    that it needs a package; it never declares which version. This is the same
    separation between rule and policy, applied to
    dependencies: the projects state what they need, one file states the terms.

    The practical payoff is that two projects cannot silently drift onto
    different versions of the same package, which is the usual way a build starts
    behaving differently depending on which project restored last.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Test framework">
    <!--
      xUnit v3. The v2 packages are what `dotnet new xunit` still scaffolds, and
      staying on them because a template said so is not a decision. v3 runs each
      test assembly as its own process, which matters here: the concurrency contract
      makes freedom from shared mutable state a contract for rule authors, and a
      runner that isolates assemblies is the one that will actually catch a
      violation instead of hiding it behind a shared AppDomain.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3" Version="4.0.0" />

    <!--
      TRX reporting under MTP. MIT, github.com/microsoft/testfx.

      Note the company it does not keep: xunit.runner.visualstudio and
      Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk are absent, because both belong to VSTest and the
      .NET 10 SDK refuses to run an MTP project through the VSTest target at all.
      MTP is not a preference here; xUnit v3 is MTP-based and the SDK forces the
      pairing.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Testing.Extensions.TrxReport" Version="2.3.3" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Assertions">
    <!--
      Shouldly, not FluentAssertions.

      FluentAssertions moved to a paid commercial licence at v8 in January 2025;
      v7 stays Apache 2.0 but is frozen. AwesomeAssertions is the community fork
      of v7 and is a reasonable choice, but it carries fork-maintenance risk with
      no commercial backing behind it.

      Shouldly has neither problem: continuously maintained, permissively
      licensed throughout its history, and its failure messages name the
      expression that failed rather than just the values. For a repository whose
      whole argument is about dependency and policy discipline, picking the
      dependency with no licence history is the consistent move.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Shouldly" Version="4.3.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Test doubles and data">
    <!--
      NSubstitute fakes the four context services:
      IFileSystem, IProcessRunner, IRuleLogger and IChangeSource. Those exist
      precisely so the built-in rules can be tested without a disk or a compiler.

      Bogus generates the bulk data those tests need — thousands of ChangedFile
      entries for a pre-submit rule, plausible paths and sizes — without a
      hand-written fixture that everyone is afraid to touch.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="NSubstitute" Version="6.2.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Bogus" Version="35.6.5" />

    <!--
      Spectre.Console.Testing drives the arrow-key picker without a terminal.
      MIT, same repository as the library it tests.

      It is here rather than absent because the alternative left the one piece of
      that feature nobody could see: the picker builds its rows, marks the active
      one, and maps the chosen label back to a value, and none of that is the
      library's code. A TestConsole with keystrokes pushed into it exercises
      exactly that and nothing else. Where the line falls: the rows, the marker
      and the mapping are this project's to get right, and the rendering below
      them belongs to the library and is not tested here.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Spectre.Console.Testing" Version="0.51.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Snapshot testing">
    <!--
      Verify covers the golden files. The console reporter is
      the one component whose correctness is entirely about exact bytes — glyphs,
      alignment, ordering, the unicode and ASCII variants — and hand-written
      string assertions over a 20-line report are unreadable and unmaintainable.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Verify.XunitV3" Version="32.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Behaviour specifications">
    <!--
      Reqnroll, the open-source successor to SpecFlow after Tricentis
      discontinued it.

      Scoped deliberately: it covers engine behaviour that a production owner
      would recognise as a rule of the system — verdict aggregation, the
      blocking-by-gating matrix, skip attribution to the root
      cause. Those read well as scenarios because they are policy, not code.
      Unit-level work stays in xUnit, where it belongs.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Reqnroll.xUnit.v3" Version="3.3.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Coverage carries NO PackageReference on purpose. It is driven entirely by two
    local tools pinned in .config/dotnet-tools.json: coverlet.console collects and
    ReportGenerator renders. Both MIT, both cross-platform, neither needing an
    account or a hosted service. See scripts/coverage.ps1.

    The usual coverlet.collector / coverlet.msbuild packages are absent because
    neither works here. Both hook the VSTest pipeline, and the .NET 10 SDK runs
    these projects through Microsoft.Testing.Platform instead. They restore and
    build cleanly and then silently produce nothing, which is worse than being
    missing: a coverage step that reports success having measured nothing is a
    green run over a check that never happened, aimed at the instrumentation
    rather than at the workspace.

    coverlet.console does not care about the runner. It instruments the assemblies
    and executes the test binary, and an xUnit v3 test project *is* a binary.
  -->

  <!--
    Runtime packages. Preflight.Abstractions still carries none and never will:
    it depends on nothing beyond the BCL, and that is verified by a test rather
    than trusted. Everything here belongs to
    Preflight.Cli, which is the only project allowed a third-party dependency.

    System.CommandLine is the argument parser. It went
    stable at 2.0.0 after years of beta, which is why the design could name it
    before it existed in a shippable form.

    One consequence to expect rather than discover: Directory.Build.props sets
    TreatWarningsAsErrors with AnalysisLevel latest-recommended, and the handler
    code System.CommandLine generates has historically produced nullable
    warnings. Those arrive here as build errors, not warnings.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Runtime">
    <PackageVersion Include="System.CommandLine" Version="2.0.11" />

    <!--
      Spectre.Console draws the arrow-key picker, and nothing else. MIT,
      github.com/spectreconsole/spectre.console.

      Written by hand it would be ReadKey, raw ANSI, terminal resize, Ctrl+C and
      restoring the terminal from an exception — a week of edge cases for one
      widget, and every one of those edge cases is a way to leave somebody's
      terminal broken after a validation run. Sharprompt is the smaller surface
      and was turned down for the reason this file exists: it has no commercial
      or foundation backing behind it, and every other entry here was chosen
      against that same bar.

      Confined to Interactive/ and never reachable from Reporting/. Two systems
      deciding the same ANSI bytes is how the golden files stop being the truth,
      and an architecture test holds the boundary.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Spectre.Console" Version="0.51.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
